                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A very short and strange point and click game, with a strange investigation in strange locations and a detective with a strange sense of humor.\nFrom the very first seconds, our investigation begins strangely, but it is precisely this strange and offbeat aspect that I liked. The music and graphics perfectly reinforce the story's surreal atmosphere. It’s an adventure that blends various themes: detective story, horror, retro sci-fi, and comedy. A great mix that I would have liked to see go further into the surreal, the adventure unfortunately ending a little too quickly.\n\nThe gameplay sticks to classic point-and-click conventions without being overly difficult.\nOne small detail I really liked: whenever you visit a new screen for the first time, the detective you control offers a brief description. These are often delivered with a comic, cynical tone that I found amusing.\n\nOn the downside, there are bugs. None of them are game-breaking, but I found myself replaying certain sequences or dialogue lines multiple times. I also managed to duplicate items I’d already used and even skipped a puzzle entirely. These bugs mostly occur towards the end of the game.\n\nA second negative point is the developers' arbitrary decision to lock off the game's first areas, even though there is no logical reason to block access to them. This stopped me from going back to complete a secondary objective required for the game's \"positive\" ending. Lacking the motivation to replay the entire game, I settled for the \"neutral\" and \"negative\" endings (there are three different endings in total).\n\nIn conclusion, despite its flaws, I still recommend Earthworms for its unique, surreal style.",
